[Music]  from its opening stage sonic forces  displays a number of issues that are  emblematic of the journey ahead its  insistent tutorial messages interrupt  your initial sprint down a winding road  the cinematic transition sequences that  take you from one path to the next  renders you an observer not an active  participant and read as you're about to  settle to the Glee of your mad dash  forward the stage ends even sonic forces  most entertaining moments come with  caveats the game's story once again sees  sonic getting involved in the battle  against dr. Eggman who is joined by a  powerful entity known as infinite to  combat the threat a ragtag group of  freedom fighters consisting of Sonic a  younger version of himself most of the  supporting cast and a new character you  personally create simply named the  rookie come together while the story's  stakes provide an interesting power  shift where dr. Eggman has the advantage  thanks to infinite it falls short of  being interesting or impactful it's only  in Sonic forces is levity where it  manages to be somewhat entertaining  turning to puns or brief comedic  situations to elicit a snicker but all  too infrequently but what's that  you reek of fear glad to see I lecture  that's not beer I ran all the way over  here throughout your adventure you'll  switch back and forth between playing is  either modern sonic classic sonic or  your custom character the biggest  addition to the formula is your custom  character who sports special weapons  called wisp ons that grant unique  offensive and movement abilities all  three characters play distinctly from  one another and there are fleeting  thrills to be had and clung through  robots with a speed boost or using a  homing attack on a series of flying  creatures to quickly clear a path  towards the finish line  however the excitement of these  high-speed escapades are held back by  clunky platforming and unwieldy movement  during platforming and speed sequences  you frequently plummet down bottomless  pits due to how abruptly you care to  build up speed before a jump or how  erodes bumpers aren't made clear while  death is to be expected the level design  repeatedly miss communicates the  placement of oncoming hazards and the  timing required to avoid them admittedly  practice means you inevitably develop  the reflexes demanded of you over time  but even with experience the game's  inconsistencies mean you'll often end up  stuck on a ramp mid run or make a double  jump that simply doesn't work the way  you want the game also does a poor job  of teaching you the flow of its design  instead relying on multiple frustrating  and unfair deaths to educate you on the  intricacies of a stages pacing there's a  pervading sense of monotony across Sonic  Forces is-7 unremarkable worlds nearly  all the obstacles you encounter are  rehashes of concepts and mechanics from  previous games a set-piece sometimes  breaks up the pace  but these encounters usually boil down  to simplistic QuickTime events that make  you feel passive to the action happening  on screen rather than an active  participant  it also doesn't help that stages are  also incredibly short typically clocking  in at two and a half minutes with  cutscenes before and after each stage  you can't help but wish there was a  little more ground to cover before  reaching the finish line your custom  characters wisp ons add some variety to  the mechanics but even those are limited  as there are only a couple that offer  practical benefits for instance the  lightning wisp on allows you to zip  through a line of rings often leading  you to alternate routes in the stage out  of the seven West ponds available you're  likely to stick to using one or two as  there's rarely any incentive to  experiment once you've grown accustomed  to how a couple work  in terms of performance sonic forces run  smoothly at 60 frames per second on ps4  Xbox one and PC the switch version  however runs at 30 frames per second and  suffers from a downgrade in visuals  comparatively while docked or undocked  it'd be fair to write sonic forces off  as another weak entry in the series its  numerous shortcomings make for an uneven  often frustrating gameplay experience  however knowledge of its various flaws  can make for a smoother more gratifying  second run-through in replaying 4's  ranks it's possible to use your  accumulated knowledge of a stages  hazards and its most illogical pitfalls  to better your experiences but  speedrunning are not sonic forces Iselle  design stages and port handling are  still major obstacles that detract from  your time spent playing  [Music]  for years the Sonic series has come up  short in its 3d games  it wasn't until Sonic Colors and Sonic  Generations that the series was able to  grasp a semblance of quality that could  change the perception of the series for  the better  sonic forces ultimately fails to advance  the mechanics of previously successful  3d Sonic games or present them in their  best light a mediocre platformer at best  sonic forces simply deepens long-held  stereotypes against Sega's beloved blue  blur  [Music]  [Music]

